I can only guess that you are not too familiar with the NAACP.  For starters,
the organization was formed largely by "whites."  It's membership is open
to all.  In fact, some local chapters even have "whites" as their leaders.

Now, you may not like the politics of the NAACP, but their membership is in no
way exclusionary--either explicitly or implicitly.

In fact, after I filed a discrimination complaint against the Student
African-American Society at SU, the group went to the local NAACP for support.
The chapter flatly rejected that request, and later supported my position
that such university-sponsored discrimination was wrong (and illegal).

Basically, the NAACP are avowed integrationists, and are fundamentally
opposed to segregation.  So I fail to see how they can be compared to a
women-only group, regarding the question of exclusion.


I also have my doubts regarding your claim that some discriminatory groups
(those which are not pc) would be summarily booted from accessing internet.
I'm not aware of any law that prohibits those accessing internet from
discriminating (on the usual grounds).  A new law would probably have to
be introduced in order for such groups to be booted.

On the other hand, federal law does prohibit various federally-funded
institutions from discriminating (on the usual grounds), but that's a whole
other ball of wax.

I doubt that any court would deem access to the internet (by itself) to
constitute "federal funding" of a group, any more than it would deem the
use of interstate highways to constitute "federal funding."


