This should be extended to say 'or university or other property.'
Someone who sends mailbombs, which not only use up University
resources but also might cause harm on the receiving machine (more
on this in a second), might do it again.  It is perfectly reasonable
to suspend an account of someone who does something like this.  Why
is mailbombing harmful?  It can cause a receiving machine's mail
spool to fill up, thus causing inconvenience and downtime at that
end.  This is analogous to theft of services or destruction of
property.  

Now, if the account in question was necessary for the student to
complete classwork, that might be a different story.  However, an
account that is used for personal e-mail and games is something that
a student who has caused willful destruction of services can do
without until disciplinary proceedings are completed.
The spirit of the 'Joint Statement' is carried through with this
action.  The student was not sent home, nor kicked out of their dorm
room, nor had their ability to do homework suspended.

(all in my own opinion, of course... not an official statement of
CCSO)
