At the risk of being flamed to a crisp, perhaps I could add
my (probably redundant) 2c worth, from an academic's viewpoint.

It would seem that, in time honoured fashion, this discussion
has involved a certain amount of muddying of the waters.
The examples of 'In Memory of Her' and the teenager coupling
with his sister video are irrelevant. If they are depictions
of actual acts of incest, rape or whatever, then presumably
they are subject to applicable state and federal laws governing
such activities or the importation into the country of
representations of such actual activities (eg, snuff videos - and
I recall a recent piece on NPR about `E-mail police' patrolling
the nets in search of offending material). However, the point is
surely, and simply, that any attempt to exclude members of a
university community from accessing such material must be regarded
as an act of censorship and a violation of academic freedom. Of course
noone (or very few) wants that kind of explicit material to be made
available to minors but faculty, students and staff are *not* minors.
Furthermore, this stuff is not being beamed down into our mail boxes
(if it is, see the ongoing discussion about privacy, sexual harassment
etc.) - to access it requires an intentional act, or rather, a number
of them corresponding to each step (in my case it involves at least
4 from logon).
Of course, the university is under no obligation to make the usenet
service available, but once it has bought into the package, as it were,
it cannot pick and choose what to make available to members of the
community.
There's an obvious analogy here with journal subscriptions: the
university is under no obligation (except perhaps a moral one) to
provide the psychology or sociology depts with the Journal of Sexual
Deviancy, but once it has agreed to take out a subsrciption it cannot
then blank out certain pages, remove certain articles or impose
restrictions as to who can have access to the journal. Let's not forget
that one person's depravity is another person's evidence. One of my
