>He sends me mail, which I ignore.  (This is customary for abusers.)
>
>He talk(1)s at me, which I ignore.  (This is customary for abusers.)
>
>He tries to call me on the phone, but my number is unlisted, or the
>listed phone number is incorrect (or busy, since I'm logged in).
>
>He logs me off.
>
>I log back in, and resume.
>
>Can he or can he not suspend my account pending due process?
>
>If he can, then explain why.

If he has sent you email, then that constitutes notfication.  If (he can
verify that) you have read it, and you have not responded, then he could
probably argue that you have failed to deny the charges against you, and
as such, he can suspend your account pending a hearing.  (See my last post
on Goss v. Lopez.)

In this case, however, I don't think the question turns on this.  I think
it turns on whether or not the case represents an "emergency."


>Does the equation change if it is only a small mail message every ten
>minutes?

It may affect the matter of whether the case is an "emergency."  If any
*repeated and continuing* abuse can be reasonably deemed an "emergency"
(which it probably can) then an immediate suspension could be justified.


