----------------------------------------------------------------------
RULES FOR USERS OF THE DATA TRANSMISSION FACILITIES OF HELSINKI
UNIVERSITY OF TECHNOLOGY

Data transmission is nowadays an important means of contact
between different parts of the academic community. Helsinki
University of Technology (HUT) aims to provide its people with
the best possible services in this respect.

In order to ensure flexible functioning, it is essential that
users follow carefully the generally approved rules for the use
of the system. HUT is an academic community that needs to be able
to trust its members. People working and studying within this
community should also prove worthy of this trust.

The following are the rules for the use for data transfer on
computers and data networks owned or controlled by HUT.

Data links are intended to be used for teaching, study, research
and administration. Other uses are prohibited. Especially, the
use of the network for commercial purposes is strictly forbidden.

Each user is responsible for his/her computer permit and the use
of the system based on it.

Outside contacts using the data transfer system of HUT are
allowed only on the basis of the user's personal computer permit.
Group permits may not be used for this purpose.

Disturbance of or damage to other users, organizations or
computers in the net is forbidden. Special care should be taken
when making outside contacts because the image of HUT as an
organization and our chances to use computer networks in the
future depend on our behaviour when using the system.

All transmissions of the type that a receiver does not generally
wish to receive are prohibited. These include, but are not
necessarily limited to anonymous letters, chain letters,
advertising and other comercial material, and the dissemination
of all types of automatically spreading mutations (viruses,
worms, etc.).

Breaking into other computers, and even attempting to do so, is
strictly forbidden.

Reading messages from the network addressed to someone else is
not allowed. If a user happens by accident to see a message
addressed to someone else, its exploitation or circulation is
forbidden.

During their use of the system, users should make an effort to
save costs and to use the capacity of the system economically.
The Computing Centre maintains recommendations of practices for
the best and most economical use.

If a user notices a gap in the security of the system, he/she
should not exploit it but immediately inform the person in charge
of the computer. Similarly, security defects that are common
knowledge should not be exploited even if their correction is
delayed. The deliberate seeking of security loopholes is
prohibited.

Data transfer links may be limited if the lines are overloaded,
expensive or if their use entails serious security risks. Limits
will be precisely located and their reasons explained.

Those in charge of the system at HUT make every effort to ensure
the confidentiality of electronic mail, but with the present
technology it cannot be guaranteed.

The transmission of confidential information through electronic
mail is not encouraged. Since applicable legislation on this
point is defective, HUT takes the position that the normal
confidentiality of mail does not apply to electronic mail, nor is
an agreement made through it legally binding.

The Computing Centre is responsible for deciding how to apply the
current rules and for informing users about them.

If a user of the system has questions regarding the use of data
transfer systems, he/she should get in touch with either the
personnel of the Computing Centre or the person in charge of the
data network in his/her sub-organization.

If an abuse of the system occurs, the Computing Centre must take
immediate action. For the duration of the investigation, the
user's permit may be withdrawn, and then the following actions
may be taken:

-	a ban on the use of computers and data links,

-	internal disciplinary measures within HUT,

-	the recovery of expenses incurred,

-	criminal charges.

APPENDIX: BASIC TERMINOLOGY

	In the following, some of the basic terms used in the
rules are defined. They are included here in order to reduce the
possibility of misunderstanding and in hope of that the
terminology of the field could be simplified to a certain extent.

computer permit

The right granted to a user, obtained e.g. by filling in an
application form, to use certain of the organization's computers.

permit (quota for use, project, user password)

The concrete account entitling to use of a computer and, for
example, permitting the permanent storage of data. The account is
tied to a particular computer.

personal permit

A permit granted to an individual on the condition that it is
used only by that person. In order to use the permit, the user is
given a personal password. This is considered sufficient to
identify the user and to allot to him/her responsibility for all
the transactions done under that permit. The user is expected to
change his/her password regularly as an additional security
measure.

group permit

A permit granted to a group of people for groupwork or a permit
where the password is known to several people.

permanent data link

A link for which a fixed sum is paid regardless of the data load.

breaking into a computer.

Making contact with a computer without the necessary permit. A
computer may have services which can be used without a special
permit. Only services so defined may be used.

abuse of a computer

The use of a computer for a purpose for which it is not intended.

security gap

A weak spot in the protection of a data system through which an
outsider could break into the machine.

electronic mail

A message passed between computer users. It may be transmitted
within one computer or between computers (the user may even be
unaware that a message has been transmitted through the
electronic mail1 ). The confidentiality of mail does not apply to
electronic mail, nor is it contractually binding. When a message
is being sent or received by electronic mail, the rules of
privacy that normally apply to that computer also apply in this
case.

sub-organization

An organization within HUT that is in data link contact with
other sub-organizations of HUT and through the Computing Centre
with outside organizations. A sub-organization must be especially
careful about its permits (e.g. its personal permits) and must
make sure the rules are obeyed.


The Computing Centre

The service organization where HUT's data transfer (among other
services) is centralized. It maintains the physical and logical
structure of the network and organizes links between HUT and
external organizations.  1 The forward mechanism may transmit a
message adressed into the some computer e.g. to Kuopio.




