assembly, which is essentially the right to broadcast to self-selected
interested parties, is a constitutionally guaranteed right in the USA.
(And in Britain?---Eddy?)  It can only be defended by taking a stand
against encroachments---and these encroachments are often legal and
regulatory.  (Remember the movement against poll taxes, which were
being used to limit franchise in the American South, with the result
that they were eventually banned in the USA---though sadly not in
Great Britain.)  This usually means agitation for legislative change.
For any but the most affluent, sufficiently publicizing such a
political battle at the state or federal level to make progress
against entrenched political interests and an entrenched bureaucracy
probably requires defiance of government regulations, resulting in
court cases.  There's nothing wrong with doing that---indeed, it would
seem to particularly mark one as a good citizen that one should be
willing to take the time, and undergo the financial risk, to do so.

   Now for a final point on "operating standards/guidelines".  In America the
   FCC has jurisdiction over what you can "broadcast" on CB radios and I
   believe you still need to apply for a license to transmit on one (someone
   correct me if I am wrong).  However, enforcement of the law is lax (maybe
   close to non-existent).  At any rate, the laws exist.  I would bet Usenet as
   a whole does not want that.  Hence my statement of a need for
   self-regulation.

The FCC regulations limit (1) commercial use of those channels.
Usenet is already quite successfully self-policing in that regard.  A
more expansive reading of "commercial use", to wit, anything that
anyone might have made a buck from (see the CB discussion below) would
destroy the net.  (2) "obscenity"---any material which right-wing
censorious swill from our great heartland, usually brainless followers
of our large organized religions, might find upsetting; though, not
remarkably, the lies and violence perpetrated by government
(nationalistic propaganda, military force) usually get away without
censorship or restraint.  As far as I've seen, The FCC does enforce
the rules---poor as they are.

   As someone recently pointed out to me, one example of a good self-regulating
   "standards organization" for a broadcast media may be "ham (amateur) radio".
   Since they deal with "international communications" (as opposed to the
   limited range of CBs) it may be even more enlightening. Perhaps people who
   know more about this could comment. 

To placate the commercial interests (those who were smart and quick
enough to buy the land on which our modern public assemblies stand, at
a dollar a foot, or a dollar a kiloHertz, or a dollar a vote), ham
radio is forbidden to carry much other than line tests, idle chat, and
emergency messages.  Hams will have their licenses pulled for
transmitting music---even their own live music.  Privacy (coded
messages) is forbidden.  The whole medium is so watered down that it's
essentially content-free.  Is that what you want to have happen to
Usenet and all the other bulletin board services?

Now, "pirate" radio---that's a mildly interesting medium.

Self-censorship born of fear is craven.

To address your extreme concern for "the law" in previous postings: Of
course we need some agreed-upon rules to create a modicum of
predictability in a large and diverse society (such as laws against
murder and robbery, laws for enforcement of certain contracts, etc.)
But here in Massachusetts we have a long and proud tradition of
defiance of unjust laws, especially those that appear intended to
support vested religious, political and economic positions.  Remember
Adams?  Thoreau?  Workers versus the police in Lawrence (right to
picket)?  Even the small-time quarrel as to whether adults should be
free to drive with seatbelts unfastened, if the damned well please?

Government, whether capitalist or communist, Republican or Democratic,
Labor or Conservative, is the domain of some of the most conservative
and ambitious people in any society---exactly those people who are
most likely to attempt to mould a neighbor's behavior by force.
EVERYONE should fight legal repression, and censorship is repression.
If you don't want your children exposed to nude adults, or to revealed
religion, or to natural science, that's very much your own problem.
I'll wish you luck, or not, as the case may be.  But if you and your
ilk manage to institute your beliefs as law, then I'll hope I have the
integrity to publicly challenge that law.

No medium is so important that we should hide our faces and ignore
political attacks on essential freedoms out of fear.  We might have
to live without Usenet.  If we live with censorship, we are slaves.
