>
>I was told by Sandy Moy, UW director of computer services, on Oct 25,
>1991 that *all* the picture groups were cut (not just the "sex" ones),
>that you needed to cut them to make more disk space, that the removal
>was planned weeks before the newspaper articles, and that "We have no
>intention of pulling files just because they have the word "sex" in
>their title...". I believe she said much the same to the _Daily_.
>
>Mr. Newell, can you clarify why "alt.sex.pictures" was dropped?

First off, Sandy Moy is director of University Computing Services, one of six
units within the UW's department of Computing and Communications (CAC), i.e.
she's but one of six directors in the department.  UCS runs a central news
server for the benefit of the campus community, currently using a DEC 5000
workstation (shelley.u.washington.edu) with a dedicated 1 Gb disk partition for
news.  Virtually all academic departments at the UW configure their readers to
point to this central server, because it has lots of disk space and is carrying
a full feed, including the UW's subscription to Clarinet.

Because so many departments at the UW depend on the one server for access to
the news feed, any policy decisions on adding or removing groups are based on
general consensus, and no one person in CAC has unilateral authority to remove
a group.  As I discussed in a previous article, there exists a policy
advisory committee to review potentially controversial decisions.  Also,
departmental net admins meet once a month to discuss planning and software
maintenance issues, and Usenet-related issues occasionally arise in that forum
as well.

The alt.sex.pictures group was removed in anticipation of a highly-critical
newspaper article in the Seattle Post-Intelligencer.  The group was pulled at
approx. 10pm on the evening of Monday, 14 October 1991.  The article appeared
the morning of Tuesday, 15 October, with many people at the UW knowing about it
the day before.  We were beseiged by the news media throughout the day of the
15th.  Other than the P-I, I don't think anyone in the media had any idea the
a.s.p group had been pulled, or even that it had ever existed on a UW system.

I was the User Services consultant in UCS responsible for Usenet support at
the time, and I knew nothing about any plan to remove any group before the
14th, even though I had been arguing for its removal for several months prior.

The claim that disk space issues motivated the removal is easily refuted.  As
I noted above, the server has a 1 Gb partition for news that is less than 50%
utilized at present, i.e. given current expirations there is at least .5 Gb
free at any given time.  A feed of a.b.p.e with a 3-5 day expiration would cost
about 20-30 Mb, on average, based on what I see in the latest traffic reports.

So, Carl, why isn't the group there now??

--
Bill Newell
wcn@u.washington.edu

