The answer to your question is yes, subject to conditions of due process.  All
rules governing disciplinary hearings are covered in the UW Handbook, with
which all campus administrators and academic advisors are expected to be
familiar.  The AUP does not attempt to duplicate procedural details already
spelled out in the Handbook.

Computer staff cannot, however, use their privileges to snoop through users'
private files without authorization from above.  Such action, if verified,
would be treated as a disciplinary offense in its own right.  A consultant may,
on occasion, look at an individual user's e-mail file WITH PRIOR PERMISSION
from that user in order to investigate a delivery problem.  When I used to do
this, I encouraged users to forward to me a headers-only copy of the problem
message, but alas this is beyond the means of some neophyte users.  A systems
programmer or consultant must have a sense of professional ethics, and must
learn to ignore the text of any private e-mail that might be inadvertently seen
in the course of troubleshooting some technical problem.

The UW doesn't even run the Arbitron program any more, at least in part because
