Inaugural Meeting of CommUnity: Minutes

This message forms the minutes of the inaugural meeting of 
CommUnity: The Computer Communicators Association, held on 
March 14, 1993 in St. Alban's Centre, London.

Our apologies if receive more than one copy of this message.
This is the second copy to be sent out, with some minor
errata corrected. Due to some problems with the mailer
on this system, I am unsure how many people the first 
version reached.

Distribution is as follows:

All Internet-addressable sites by Eddy Robinson
BBS.Freedom conference on CIX  "  "    "
uk.announce Usenet newsgroup   "  "    "

All FIDOnet Sites by Malcolm Arnold
FIDOnet echoes    "  "       "

Compuserve Forums by Nigel Hardy

Other sites not mentioned above by individual members.

Queries or commments can be addressed to Eddy Robinson at the 
following addresses, as can any general enquiries about CommUnity
until the commitee appoints a secretary:

	baphomet@cix.compulink.co.uk
	baphomet@temple.demon.co.uk
=====================================================================
Contents
=====================================================================
0.        Preamble
1.        Proceedings
2.        List of Attendees


=====================================================================
0.        Preamble
=====================================================================
First, apologies for the slight delay in releasing these minutes.
Rather than knock out a quick summary I opted to keep all the  
information in one message, which obviously took a little longer to 
prepare.

Due to the length of this text, I have opted not to include all the 
details of debate, since these were a) lengthly in places, and 
b) unanimously agreed to be encompassed by the actual resoltions carried.
All resolutions were carried overwhelmingly. A full record of all 
contributions and comments is available to anyone who wants a copy.

Irrelevancies such as tea breaks and so on are also not mentioned.  We 
would like to give thanks to St. Alban's Church for allowing us to use 
the room on a Sunday.


=====================================================================
1.        Proceedings
=====================================================================
Event:        Inaugural Meeting of CommUnity: The Computer
	      Communicators Association.
Date:         14th March 1993
Venue:        St. Alban's Centre, Holborn, London.

Present:      See list of attendees at end.
Chairman:     David Cluley
Secretary:    Eddy Robinson/Neil Ford.

Proceedings:-


It was resolved that a formal body should be set up, with aims as 
detailed below:

A. IMMEDIATE AIMS
-----------------
1. Establish, maintain and connect a membership of technology and
   computer-based communications users who share a concern that
   access to technology, information and communication should be as
   freely available as possible.

2. Monitor and inform press and media coverage of technology and
   (principally) computer-based communications, responding to
   misinformation and prejudice with a coherent voice.

3. Raise public awareness about civil liberties issues
   (such as freedom of speech, communication and expression) arising
   from the rapid advancement in the area of computer-based
   communications media.

4. Develop among policy-makers a better understanding of the
   issues underlying free and open telecommunications, and support
   - by whatever means available to us - the creation of legal and
   structural approaches which will both ease the assimilation of
   these new technologies by society, and maintain access to them in
   line with the vision inherently expressed by the aims in this
   Mission Statement.

5. Connect with, and help to network, agencies and individuals
   (both in the UK and globally), who might share our concerns and
   hopes for the development of technology and computer-based
   communications as tools for the sharing of information and
   resources, and for social activity.


B. EVENTUAL AIMS
----------------
1. Extend our membership and organisation to include
   wider-Europe, or assist there in the establishment and networking
   of independent groups sharing our concerns, aims and vision.

2. Support litigation in the public interest to preserve,
   protect, and extend civil rights within the realm of computing
   and telecommunications technology.

3. Support and engage in educational activities which increase
   popular understanding of the opportunities and challenges posed
   by developments in computing and telecommunications.

4. Encourage and support the development of new tools which will
   endow non-technical users with full and easy access to
   computer-based telecommunications.



It was resolved that membership be open to both organisations and 
individuals, on the basis of one member, one vote.
Categories of and fees for membership to be decided by commitee and 
reviewed as necessary.

The meeting was informed of various approaches to funding in combination
maintaining a democratic approach.


After lengthly debate, it was resolved to adopt the name: 

	CommUnity
	The Computer Communicators Association

in the form above.


The following were proposed and seconded for membership of the commitee,
and a short resume of who they are was also given:-

Alistair Kelman        [akelman@cix.compulink.co.uk]
	Senior member of the bar. Currently handling ATM case against the
	banks; represented Steve Gold & Robert Schifreen when they were
	prosecuted for hacking.

Eddy Robinson
	Freelance journalist/contractor. Wrote PC Magazine article on FAST/
	ELSPA regulation proposals, moderates bbs.freedom conference on CIX.

Maclolm Arnold
	BBS Sysop. Edits and publishes "The Message", a quarterly magazine
	dedicated to communications issues, and moderates the Cyberspace 
	echo on Fido.

Stuart Dyckhoff
	Representing the GT Power BBS system.

Dave Winder
	Journalist, organiser. Writes on computer communications and 
	disability issues, and is active in these fields.

Nigel Hardy
	BBS Sysop. Runs the Arkham BBS system, one of the best established
	Fido Net bulletin boards.

Len Stuart
	Representing Netreach.

Peter Ceresole
	Television producer. Has been involved withcurrent affairs 
	programmes such as Panorama. Soon going freelance as a producer.
	
Ian Jackson
	.

Pat Winstanley         [peewee@cix.compulink.co.uk]
	Freelance Journalist. Moderates the FAST&ELSPA echo on FidoNet and
	is a co-moderator of the bbs.freedom conference on CIX.

David Cluley
	Voluntary sector. Long experience with both computing and charity/
	voluntary work.

Dylan Harris
	Member of the electoral reform society: has run elections for the
	Green party and others.


2 to-be-filled places for representatives of JANET, the Joint Academic 
NETwork.

[ E-mail addresses are supplied for those unable to be present and thus 
  not in the attendees list. ]

A block vote was taken on the suitability ofthe commitee asawhole and 
was carried unanimously.


It was resolved that said commitee had the authority to co-opt people 
as its members saw fit, and the responsibility that networksare 
represented, where possible.


It was agreed that a draft constitution be produced and distributed by 
the first of July, incorporating discussions on the networks, with 
best efforts.


It was agreed that another physical meeting take place at the beginning 
of September 1993, with a time and place to be arranged and published by 
the commitee by 14th April.


It was resolved that action be taken to set up a Usenet Newsgroup under 
the uk.* hierachy, and gatewayed to as many systems as possible. 
Organisation to be handled by the commitee, and that a regular digest 
of proceedings be issued.


It was agreed that a voluntary donation of 5 pounds could be made to
cover expenses until the next meeting. Details (adddresses etc) to 
be announced with the details of the Septemmber meeting. Donations 
from those present were collected.


The meeting was informed of the first "European Computers Freedom & 
Privacy" conference, to be held in London November 1993. Mail 
ecfp-request@demon.co.uk for further details.


It was communicated to the commitee that they should consider a code 
of conduct for on-line systems.


Meeting closed.
