 ACTIVISM IN ACADEMIA
 Department of Politics and Peace and Justice Studies Have an Agenda

 taken from Campus Report, Accuracy In Academia, June 1991

  The University of San Francisco is a Jesuit-run institution just
 blocks from Haight-Ashbury, the center of the counter-culture
 movement in the 1960s.  With the changes in the political science
 classes at USF, students can learn about the kind of politics that
 made Haight-Ashbury famous in _Politics of the Sixties,_ and
 participate in it through university sponsored internships and
 classes like _Political Participation and Global Movements._
  At USF, the Peace and Justice Studies Program (PJS) has a
 brochure advertising its classes that reads: _The dramatic events
 recently unfolding in Eastern Europe and elsewhere in the world
 should encourage us.  New possibilities for peace and justice have
 emerged.  We_re in a time of great potential and change.
 Nevertheless, formidable forces still impede a more just and
 peaceful world....Racism and sexism plague our society and the
 world community....Drug wars create alarming victimization and
 perpetuate military establishments.  Environmental decay has
 reached crisis proportions.
  _With both a sense of hope and a sense of continuing concern, the
 Department of Politics sponsors the Peace and Justice Studies
 Program.  The university provides a base for understanding and
 solving global problems; PJS helps focus those efforts._
  The chairman of the Peace and Justice Studies Program is Robert
 Elias, a professor in the Department of Politics.  The Fall, 1990,
 course catalog notes that Elias has _worked in the U.S. peace and
 anti-intervention movements, and traveled in Latin America, Europe,
 and Africa,_ as well as serving as a researcher at Oxfam, the
 Institute for Defense and Disarmament, and the Vera Institute of
 Justice.  He also serves as the associate editor of Peace Review.
  During Spring semester of 1991, Elias taught the Politics courses
 _Controversies in Constitutional Law,_ _Human Rights and Global
 Change,_ and _Political Participation and Global Movements._
 _Political Participation,_ part of the PJS, has a course
 description that reads: _Theory and practice of political activism
 and movements (women_s, black freedom, labor, socialist,
 environmental, etc.) for democracy, justice, and political change.
 Ranges from domestic participation (acting locally) to broader
 global movements (thinking globally)._  _Constitutional Law_ dealt
 with _Constitutional rights vs. political and economic power,_ and
 planned to deal with affirmative action, the death penalty,
 abortion, flag burning, war power, defendant_s rights, pornography,
 and drug testing.
  Elias_s views on drugs are well known at USF.  In a three-part
 series called _Victims of the Drug Wars_ that was published in the
 USF student newspaper, the San Francisco Foghorn last year, Elias
 blasted the drug control policies of the Bush Administration,
 writing: _Our drug policies curb our rights, and victimize the
 poor, minorities, the ill, women, children, drug-users, crime
 victims, foreigners, even police.  Although painful to admit,
 they_re less wars against drugs, and more wars against victims and
 political change._  While claiming that _drug wars promote racism,_
 Elias also charged: _Drug wars legitimize U.S. military
 interventions abroad, replacing the declining Cold War.  They
 promote continued CIA subversion, assassinations and
 counterinsurgency.  They maintain foreign economic dependence and
 subservience, perpetuating pliant dictators and favorable business
 climates for narrow U.S. interests._
  Elias was equally vocal in his resentment toward government
 policies during Operation Desert Storm, reportedly saying Bush_s
 actions were based on a _penis envy_ of Saddam Hussein.  In one
 editorial he wrote for the Foghorn, he warned: _The government uses
 patriotism to manipulate us, pushing us toward supporting the
 unbelievable abominations that fill the sad bloody history of U.S.
 foreign policy.  We must not let the government define patriotism
 for us....Patriotism is not a matter of supporting immoral
 policies, but rather humane ones._  He also claimed: _Our recent
 presidents have ruled as monarchs, with our political processes
 serving only as ritual window-dressing to presidential and
 corporate domination._
  In another anti-war editorial, Elias attempted to recruit USF
 into the anti-war movement as a whole, writing: _We should oppose
 this war at USF because it contravenes the Jesuit mission of
 promoting peace and justice, which can_t be reached through war and
 violence.  Also, for too long, universities, despite occasional
 protests, have either acquiesced or promoted militarism in U.S.
 foreign policy.  This must stop: universities must, as
 institutions, regain their purpose of using knowledge for
 productive, not destructive, objectives.  We must not be a party to
 this immoral war._
  Elias and three other members of the Department of Politics (out
 of a total of five professors) signed onto a _stop the war petition
 against the Persian Gulf War_ that appeared in the February 27th
 edition of the Foghorn.  The petition called for an immediate
 cease-fire and withdrawal of troops, and _The [United Nations] must
 immediately launch an international peace conference to provide a
 negotiated solution to the Persian Gulf Crisis, the
 Isreali-Palestine conflict, and other sources of tension in the
 region._
  Elias was the recipient of USF_s National Endowment for the
 Humanities Chair for 1990-91 for his project _Resistance Literature
 in Eastern Europe and Latin America."
  The Department of Politics recently changed it_s name from the
 Department of Government, explaining the change _because our
 courses encompass not merely the traditional examination of
 government structures and processes, but also the broader politics
 influencing our society._  Among the changes in the department have
 been the formation of two new programs: the Public Service Program
 and Peace and Justice Studies.
  The PJS brochure talks of getting students involved in political
 activism, stating: _We_d like to promote a more active and engaged
 USF student body.  We expose students systematically to global
 issues, and prepare them for a more meaningful, global citizenship,
 emphasizing the things they might contribute, directly or
 indirectly, to peace and justice in our society and the world._
 According to the course schedule, students participating in PJS are
 offered _internship[s] with peace, human rights, or community
 organizations._  One organization, Neighbor to Neighbor, has
 sponsored a boycott of coffee from El Salvador in an effort to
 force the democratically-elected government to enter negotiations
 with the Marxist FMLN guerrillas.
  The cover of the course schedules for the Department of Politics
 is a compilation of political figures on the lawn of the White
 House, prominently showing Ronald Reagan holding Margaret
 Thatcher_s hand with Nancy Reagan at his side.  Behind the Reagans
 are Lt. Colonel Oliver North, Fawn Hall (North_s secretary), George
 Bush in a tuxedo with money coming out of his breast pocket,
 columnist George Will, and Saudi King Fahd.  Thatcher has, among
 others, Chile_s General Pinochet and Adolf Hitler standing behind
 her.  In the background, Jesse Jackson, Fidel Castro,  Karl Marx,
 and others are seen clustered around a tree behind the main group.
 Also, the American flag atop the White House is bent, most likely
 from the lightning flashing around it.
 Besides Elias, other professors in the department have been
 described by one student who contacted Campus Report as _total
 communist_ and _radical feminist environmentalist._  The one
 professor who reportedly kept his class balanced, Donald Braden,
 recently resigned from the faculty, tendering what some have called
 a _terse_ letter of resignation.
  The changes in the Department of Politics come at a time when
 many other problems are facing the university community, including
 a threat to it_s accreditation.  The Western Association of Schools
 and Colleges released a report in 1989 noting faculty burnout, low
 productivity, and _how seldom intentions and objectives lead to
 desired results_ at USF.  The visiting team from WASC reported:
 _What worried us was the feeling that many changes at USF are not
 in the service of achieving a vision but are done for more
 expedient reasons._  The report also noted: _What was less evident
 was the embodiment of that Jesuit vision in a larger vision of USF
 in the broad university tradition, and a sense of itself as a
 special and unique institution._  Veteran Professor Michael Lehmann
 wrote in an editorial: _Not only are we [USF] in dire fiscal
 straits, but we have slipped from being a good second-rate
 institution to a troubled third-rate institution._
  Others think that the changes in USF threaten the school on a
 moral level.  In a letter to the Foghorn, Kalina Howenstein wrote:
 "By satisfying and giving into these demands, which are
 inconsistent with the school's Catholic heritage, the university is
 permitting itself to be mainpulated by a  few radicals who want to
 destroy the school's traditional and moral values."
  As of 1991, USF has also lost the support a significant number of
 students after dubious spending was alleged by the Foghorn.  Steve
 Ferry, the Foghorn_s managing editor, wrote: _A university is
 intended to educate its students, not betray them.  We pay
 escalating tuition to receive a degree not to be lied to._
 Sugiarto Swandi, responding to an opinion survey, told the paper
 that _for the past three years the students have seen nothing but
 incompetence and mismanagement of our tuition dollars and
 government grants._
  This distrust stems from the financial affairs of Vice President
 for Student Development Carmen Jordan-Fox.  Jordan-Fox had been
 alleged to have actively participated in the plans for the
 remodeling of a house acquired by the university that she was
 thinking of moving into, as well as being appointed as an interim
 co-athletic director and receiving half the salary.  When student
 government President Pete Saari and others asked the administration
 to explain the situation, they were initially told the matter was
 _none of your business._
  Pressure from letters to the editor and elsewhere prompted USF_s
 president, Father John Lo Schiavo, S.J., to release an open letter
 to the university community downplaying the controversy, saying
 that Jordan-Fox would not be moving into the house (and only picked
 out the color of the paint) and he made the decision to appoint her
 to the athletic position with _justified financial compensation._
